  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Away from the town centre, Les Salins is a family-friendly beach of fine sand and clear water, away from the resort's bustle (regional tourist office). Depending on the currents, dry posidonia seagrass may pile up there; this seagrass is harmless. It is accessible to people with reduced mobility from the La Moutte park, and a coastal path leads to a quieter cove.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>As on all the town's beaches, municipal order no. 1828/2026 of 6 July 2026 allows dogs on the town's beaches from 6am to 9.30am and 6.30pm to 10pm, from 16 July to 15 September 2026; outside those hours they are not allowed. Dogs must stay under their owner's constant responsibility and be kept on a leash when approaching other users and whenever circumstances require; the order does not apply to walkers with dogs on a leash on the coastal path. It only covers that period: for the rest of the year, we found no published rule, so rely on the signage on site. The regional tourism listing (Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur Tourisme), updated in September 2025, says "animals not accepted", with no mention of hours — so probably predating the July 2026 order, but that isn't guaranteed. Check the signage on site before settling in with your dog.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: in summer, come with your dog in one of the two allowed windows (6am-9.30am or 6.30pm-10pm) and bring fresh water.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>To the west, a <a href="/en/fiche-balade-sentier-littoral-moutte-salins-saint-tropez">coastal path</a> links Les Salins to the point of <a href="/en/fiche-plage-moutte-saint-tropez">La Moutte</a>, going around the rocky headland.</p>
